@meta
id: 08-01
title: Delete one character where you stand
chapter: 08
type: lesson
skills: delete-d
par: 12
minutes: 3
vscode: click to the right of the character, press Backspace
open: invoice.ts

@brief
Everything so far has been movement. From here on you get verbs, and the
first one is the smallest: remove the character under the cursor, without
entering insert mode, without a selection, without the mouse.

A merge went badly and two lines ended up with a doubled semicolon.
TypeScript compiles it, the linter does not. Take out the extra one on
each line.

You already know how to get to the end of a line. That is the only motion
you need here.

@keys
| `x` | delete the character under the cursor |
| `$` | last character of the line |
| `j` | down a line |

@hints
The character you want gone is the last one on the line, so land on it
first and then deal with it.
--
`x` deletes the character the cursor is sitting on. The cursor stays
where it is; the rest of the line shuffles left.
--
`$` puts you on the second semicolon. `x` removes it. `j` then `$` does
the same on the next line.

@solution
    j $ x  j $ x  :wq<Enter>

Ten keystrokes and no mode changes at all. That is the shape of every
verb in this chapter: you are in normal mode before the edit and in
normal mode after it, which means the next command is ready immediately.

`x` is the one people reach for out of habit long after they should have
stopped. It is right for one stray character. It is wrong for a word, a
line or a block, and pressing it eleven times to clear an argument is a
sign you needed one of the keys later in this chapter.

There is a backwards twin, `X`, which deletes the character *before* the
cursor — the Backspace you never have to leave normal mode for. The next
lesson uses it.

@start invoice.ts
export function formatTotal(cents: number) {
  const pounds = cents / 100;;
  const label = `£${pounds.toFixed(2)}`;;

  return label;
}

@expected invoice.ts
export function formatTotal(cents: number) {
  const pounds = cents / 100;
  const label = `£${pounds.toFixed(2)}`;

  return label;
}
